    <!-- Add Activity -->

    <p>
        <term> A relation</term> establishes a connection between elements of a starting set (the inputs) and elements of a destination set (the outputs). In mathematics, the behavior of a relation depends entirely on how these inputs and outputs are mapped to one another.
    </p>
    <p>
        Some relations assign exactly one unique output to each input, while others might map a single input to multiple outputs, or multiple inputs to the same output. Recognizing these mapping patterns is a crucial step in determining whether a specific relation qualifies as a function.
    </p>

    <activity xml:id="activity-exploring-relations">

        <introduction>
            <p>
                Examine the following sets of ordered pairs. Discuss with a partner what you notice about how the input values (<m>x</m>-coordinates) and output values (<m>y</m>-coordinates) are connected.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <p>
                Determine whether each relation represents:
            </p>
            <p>
                <ul>
                <li><p>One-to-One</p></li>
                <li><p>Many-to-One</p></li>
                <li><p>One-to-Many</p></li>
                <li><p>Many-to-Many</p></li>
            </ul>
            </p>

            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                <li>
                    <md>\{(1,2), (2,4), (3,6)\}</md>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <md>\{(1,5), (2,5), (3,5)\}</md>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <md>\{(1,2), (1,4), (1,6)\}</md>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <md>\{(1,2), (1,3), (2,3), (2,4)\}</md>
                </li>
            </ol>
            </p>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Explain the reasoning you used to identify each type of relation. Based on your understanding, which of the relations listed above are functions?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

