    <!-- Add insight / key takeaway directly related to activity / learner experience -->

    <insight>
        <paragraphs xml:id="para-relation-concepts">
            <title>Conceptualizing Types of Relations</title>
            <p>
                To classify a relation, we must examine the specific pathways from inputs to outputs. A <term>one-to-one relation</term> is the most direct mapping: each individual input is connected to exactly one unique output, meaning no two inputs ever share the same output value. 
            </p>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            For example, a marriage is a one-to-one relation between one person and exactly one spouse. Each person is married to only one other person, and no two people share the same spouse. This strict pairing is what defines a one-to-one relation.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
                In contrast, a <term>many-to-one relation</term> occurs when two or more different inputs are connected to the exact same output value. Because each input still only points to a single destination, both one-to-one and many-to-one relations satisfy the strict requirement to be classified as functions.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            For example, a child-to-mother relationship is a many-to-one relation. Multiple children can share the same mother, but each child has only one mother (exluding step mothers). This means that while different inputs (the children) can map to the same output (the mother), each input still corresponds to exactly one output, making it a function.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
                The rules change when an input points to multiple destinations. A <term>one-to-many relation</term> happens when a single input value is paired with two or more different output values.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            For example, the relation between borrowers and books in a library can be one-to-many. A single borrower (input) may check out multiple books (outputs), but each book is only checked out to one borrower at a time. Because one input maps to multiple outputs, this relation does not meet the definition of a function.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
                Similarly, a <term>many-to-many relation</term> is a complex mapping where several inputs connect to multiple outputs, and multiple outputs trace back to various inputs. 
            </p>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            For example, the relationship between students and courses in a school is many-to-many. A single student (input) can enroll in multiple courses (outputs), and each course can have many students enrolled. This interwoven mapping of inputs to outputs means that many-to-many relations also fail to meet the criteria for functions.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
                Because a single input can yield more than one result, <alert>neither</alert> one-to-many nor many-to-many relations are functions.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </insight>

    <paragraphs xml:id="para-line-tests-concepts">
        <title>Visualizing Relations: The Line Tests</title>
        <p>
            When a relation is graphed on a coordinate plane, we can quickly determine its properties using visual tests. 
        </p>
        <definition xml:id="def-vertical-line-test">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The <term>Vertical Line Test</term> states that a graph represents a function if and only if no vertical line intersects the graph at more than one point. If any vertical line cuts the graph multiple times, it means one input (<m>x</m>-value) maps to multiple outputs (<m>y</m>-values).
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>
        <definition xml:id="def-horizontal-line-test">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The <term>Horizontal Line Test</term> is used on a known function to check for uniqueness. A function is one-to-one if and only if no horizontal line intersects its graph at more than one point. If a horizontal line crosses multiple times, it means different inputs share the same output (making it many-to-one).
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>
    </paragraphs>

    <!-- Add further instruction (not in a block) to develop ideas as needed -->

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- Add at least 3 examples -->
    <example xml:id="ex-identifying-relations">
        <statement>
            <p>Determine the type of relation for each of the following sets of ordered pairs, and state whether the relation is a function.</p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="a">
                <li><m>\{(1,2), (2,4), (3,6), (4,8)\}</m></li>
                <li><m>\{(1,5), (2,5), (3,5)\}</m></li>
                <li><m>\{(2,1), (2,3), (2,5)\}</m></li>
                <li><m>\{(1,2), (1,3), (2,3), (2,4)\}</m></li>
            </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                
            <ol marker="a">
                <li>
                    <p>Each input is paired with one unique output, and no output value is repeated. This is a <term>one-to-one relation</term> (and it is a function).</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>The different inputs (<m>1</m>, <m>2</m>, and <m>3</m>) are all mapped to the same output value of <m>5</m>. This is a <term>many-to-one relation</term> (and it is a function).</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>A single input value (<m>2</m>) is connected to three different output values. This is a <term>one-to-many relation</term> (<alert>not</alert> a function).</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>The input <m>1</m> is paired with multiple outputs, and the output <m>3</m> is mapped from multiple inputs. This is a <term>many-to-many relation</term> (<alert>not</alert> a function).</p>
                </li>
            </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-vertical-line-test">
        <statement>
            <p>Determine whether the graph of the circle defined by <m>x^2 + y^2 = 9</m> represents a function.</p>
            
            <figure xml:id="fig-vlt-circle">
                <caption>The Vertical Line Test on a circle.</caption>
                <image width="50%" xml:id="img-vlt-circle">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth]
                            \draw[help lines, color=gray!30, dashed] (-4,-4) grid (4,4);
                            \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (-4,0) -- (4,0) node[right] {$x$};
                            \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (0,-4) -- (0,4) node[above] {$y$};
                            \draw[ultra thick, blue] (0,0) circle (3cm);
                            \draw[thick, red, dashed, &lt;-&gt;] (1.5, -4) -- (1.5, 4) node[above right] {$x=1.5$};
                            \filldraw[red] (1.5, 2.598) circle (3pt) node[right] {Intersection 1};
                            \filldraw[red] (1.5, -2.598) circle (3pt) node[right] {Intersection 2};
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>As shown in the graph, if you draw a vertical line (such as <m>x = 1.5</m>), it intersects the circle at two distinct points. Because it fails the vertical line test, the relation is <alert>not</alert> a function. One input maps to multiple outputs.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-horizontal-line-test">
        <statement>
            <p>Determine whether the function <m>f(x) = x^2</m> is one-to-one.</p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-hlt-parabola">
                <caption>The Horizontal Line Test on a parabola.</caption>
                <image width="50%" xml:id="img-hlt-parabola">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth]
                            \draw[help lines, color=gray!30, dashed] (-4,-1) grid (4,5);
                            \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (-4,0) -- (4,0) node[right] {$x$};
                            \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (0,-1) -- (0,5) node[above] {$y$};
                            \draw[ultra thick, blue, domain=-2.2:2.2, samples=50] plot (\x, {\x*\x});
                            \draw[thick, red, dashed, &lt;-&gt;] (-4, 4) -- (4, 4) node[below left] {$y=4$};
                            \filldraw[red] (-2, 4) circle (3pt) node[above left] {Intersection 1};
                            \filldraw[red] (2, 4) circle (3pt) node[above right] {Intersection 2};
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>While the graph of <m>f(x) = x^2</m> passes the vertical line test (meaning it is a function), a horizontal line such as <m>y = 4</m> intersects the parabola at two distinct points: <m>(-2, 4)</m> and <m>(2, 4)</m>. Because it fails the horizontal line test, the function is <alert>not</alert> one-to-one (it is many-to-one).</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

